---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, organizationJsonLd, webPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/about/`;
const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'About', url: canonical },
  ]),
  organizationJsonLd({
    id: SITES.main,
    name: 'Charcoal Hub',
    description: site.description,
  }),
  webPageJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    type: 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About Charcoal Hub',
    description:
      'Who operates Charcoal Hub, how supplier and product evidence is labelled, and what the platform does not claim.',
    dateModified: '2026-11-20',
    mainEntityId: SITES.main,
    siteId: SITES.main,
  }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="About Charcoal Hub"
  description="Who operates Charcoal Hub, how supplier and product evidence is labelled, and what the platform does not claim."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>About Charcoal Hub</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A B2B charcoal sourcing and product intelligence platform — not a single factory, not a blog,
      not a marketplace of unverified listings.
    </p>
    <div class="page-title-media">
      <img src="/images/page-about.webp" alt="Coconut shells, charcoal briquettes and shipping containers — supply chain conceptual illustration" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What We Do</h2>
      <p>
        We connect global B2B buyers with charcoal manufacturers in China through a standardized
        specification model, batch testing and transparent verification — every supplier profile
        carries a published verification status. Our focus is transparency, standardization and
        sourcing efficiency.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means a buyer can start with a product page, check the structured record
        behind it, read how a claimed parameter is measured, and then ask for a quotation for a
        defined specification. The commercial page is the entry point; the record and the method are
        the evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>Operating Entity</h2>
      <p>
        This platform is operated by <strong>Yancheng Xuanbao Environmental Technology Co., Ltd.</strong>
        (Xuanbao Environmental), based in Yancheng, Jiangsu, China, founded 2020-05-12.
        Sales contact: Joanna D., Sales Director, <a href="tel:+8615153588090">+86 151 5358 8090</a>,
        <a href="mailto:joanna@dinweys.com">joanna@dinweys.com</a> (English / 中文).
      </p>
      <p>
        The sales mailbox is hosted on <strong>dinweys.com</strong>, the shared mail domain used by
        our industrial-supply operations. It is the same Charcoal Hub sales desk — not a separate
        seller, agent or reseller. Quotations and order documents are issued in the name of the
        operating entity above.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are a sourcing and product-intelligence platform, not a single factory. The manufacturers
        whose products we source are independent suppliers, and each one carries a published
        verification status on the
        <a href={`${SITES.manufacturer}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">manufacturer sub-site</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>How We Handle Evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Every claim on this network is meant to be readable at three levels: the commercial page, the
        structured record and the source. Numbers that come from a supplier are labelled as
        supplier-reported. Numbers that come from a published standard or industry reference are
        labelled as such. Numbers that nobody has supplied stay <em>data pending</em> — they are not
        filled with a plausible default.
      </p>
      <p>
        Supplier profiles follow the same rule. A profile marked <em>Unverified</em> is a lead record,
        not a recommendation. <em>Verified</em> means identity and company documents were reviewed
        against a source — it is not a product-quality certificate. <em>Third-Party Verified</em>
        covers only the parameters named in the independent document. No profile is promoted because
        a supplier asked for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full rulebook — status definitions, field labels, what we refuse to publish and how to
        challenge a claim — is on the
        <a href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial &amp; Data Standards</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>What We Are Not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not the factory.</strong> Production is carried out by independent suppliers; the profile states what has been checked about each one.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a laboratory.</strong> We explain test methods and publish a report register; we do not manufacture test results.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a certification body.</strong> A verification status describes document review, not accreditation.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a marketplace with escrow.</strong> Buyers contract with the named entity and should confirm the payment route before transferring funds.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Our Principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Data first, evidence first.</strong> We publish only what we can source and verify.</li>
        <li><strong>No fabricated data.</strong> Product, manufacturer and test data are labeled by source and verification status.</li>
        <li><strong>Buyer intent over traffic.</strong> Every page answers a real buyer question or serves a commercial function.</li>
        <li><strong>Corrections over defence.</strong> A claim that cannot be supported is corrected or removed, not reworded.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Platform</h2>
      <p>
        This main site handles commercial conversion. Structured product and specification data live on
        the <a href={`${SITES.data}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">data sub-site</a>; manufacturer
        intelligence on the <a href={`${SITES.manufacturer}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">manufacturer sub-site</a>;
        testing methodology on the <a href={`${SITES.testing}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">testing sub-site</a>;
        and buyer education on the <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">knowledge hub</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The split is deliberate. A product page should not pretend to be a database, a database should
        not read like sales copy, and a test-method page should not imply that a report exists. Keeping
        those jobs separate makes each one easier to check.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact and Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        For a quotation, use the <a href="/request-quote/">quote request</a> route with the product,
        size, volume, packaging and destination port. For a physical check first, use
        <a href="/request-sample/">request samples</a>. For a factual correction, email
        <a href="mailto:joanna@dinweys.com?subject=Correction%20request">joanna@dinweys.com</a> with
        the page URL and the source that contradicts the claim.
      </p>
      <p class="updated">
        Page last reviewed 2026-11-20. Entity and contact details are repeated on the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
